ver Resampler handle similar ratios successfully in VLM literature. So 38% for M=32 and 48% for M=64 seem reasonable — they'd show that VTR is genuinely useful but hits different ceilings depending on the connector type, which is actually a valuable insight for the paper. Now I'm thinking through how this compares to random pruning at the same compression level. The comparison is becoming clearer: VTC with M=32 achieves 38% success rate while only keeping 32 tokens (87.5% compression), vastly outperforming random pruning at k=0.3 which keeps 77 tokens but only reaches 20.6%. Even at M=64, VTC's 48% success rate with just 64 tokens beats random pruning's performance at higher token counts, suggesting VTC's tier-based selection is dramatically more efficient than random token retention.\n\nNow I'm reconsidering the predictions for M=96 and M=128 in the VTC-only case, since SmolVLA showed a dip at M=96 compared to M=64—a statistically significant effect. For OpenVLA, I might see a similar pattern where increasing M doesn't monotonically improve performance, possibly due to overfitting despite VTC-only avoiding the LLM's overfitting issues.\n\nI'm settling on M=32: 38%, M=64: 48%, M=96: 52% to show a reasonable upward trend with diminishing returns. Now I'm moving to the full d108 L2-norm pruning sweep and reviewing the existing random pruning baseline results across different sparsity levels.\n\nLooking at the L2-norm predictions, they consistently fall within 5 percentage points of the random pruning results across all sparsity levels—this suggests that for MLP projectors, importance scoring doesn't meaningfully differentiate tokens since they all carry similar mixed information.\n\nFor the X-VLA seed replications, I'm seeing k=0.7 at 54% (within the expected range) and k=0.9 at 96% (close to the original), which validates the approach. The c5 RoboFlamingo experiment with its Perceiver Resampler cross-attention mechanism is unlikely to finish before the deadline, but 